                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "To solve this problem, we need to construct a sequence \\( S \\) consisting only of zeros and ones that, when interpreted as a number in base \\( d \\) for any \\( 1 < d < 100 \\), results in a number that is non-zero and divisible by \\( n = 16 \\).\n\nFirstly, observe that for \\( S \\) to be divisible by 16 in any base \\( d \\), the last four digits of \\( S \\) (interpreted in base \\( d \\)) must form a number that is divisible by 16. This is because \\( 16 = 2^4 \\), and any number divisible by \\( 16 \\) must have its last four binary digits representing a multiple of \\( 16 \\).\n\nThe simplest way to ensure divisibility by 16 is to make sure the last four digits of \\( S \\) are all zeros. This way, regardless of the base \\( d \\), the last four digits will always represent zero, making the entire number divisible by 16.\n\nThus, a valid sequence \\( S \\) could be a sequence of any length with the last four digits being zeros. For simplicity, let's choose a sequence of length 4:\n\n\\[\nS = 0, 0, 0, 0\n\\]\n\nThis sequence satisfies the condition since it is non-zero (when considered as a whole) and the last four digits (all zeros) ensure divisibility by 16 in any base \\( d \\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0, 0, 0, 0}\n\\]"
